From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:36:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/22] Metal backend: runtime-compiled gridding kernels behind
 WSCLEAN_MX_METAL

Infrastructure for the experimental Apple Metal backend, off by default.

* metal/mxshaders.h: Metal Shading Language sources compiled at runtime by the
  OS Metal runtime, so no Xcode or metallib toolchain is required. One
  threadgroup per 32x32 output tile, accumulated in threadgroup memory and
  merged with device atomics; float adds use bit-cast CAS because Apple GPUs
  have no native float atomics.
* metal/metaldevice.{h,mm}: plain-C++ interface over Objective-C++; device
  info, host-side visibility bucketing, gridding dispatch, and a
  kernel-accuracy probe (fast math vs the precise namespace).
* metal/probe_main.cpp -> mx-metal-probe: differential self-test against a
  float64 CPU twin of the same arithmetic, plus structural checks (every
  reference cell non-zero on the GPU, no cell touched without contributions)
  and exact-tie visibilities pinning the round-half-away-from-zero rule.

The probe found a substantive placement divergence: forming u + G/2 in float32
rounds at the magnitude of G/2, which flips the nearest integer for
visibilities within half a float32 step of a half-integer cell (~1 in 1e4
uniformly drawn), moving a whole 6x6 footprint one cell away from the
double-precision result and producing a 3.4e-2 relative grid error. Computing
round(u) and fx = u - round(u) in centred coordinates is exact in float32 and
removes the divergence; rel_max is 3.4e-7 afterwards.

Measured on Apple M2, unoptimized baseline with direct kernel evaluation:
200k visibilities on 1024^2 in 2.56 ms (78 Mvis/s), 1M in 11.61 ms
(86 Mvis/s), 2M on 2048^2 in 18.26 ms (110 Mvis/s), all rel_max <= 3.6e-7.

CMake: the new WSCLEAN_MX_METAL option (OFF) enables OBJCXX and metal/; every
other build is unchanged.

